Arduino Nano + NRF24 Alternative

Overview
- `Remote_Control`: battery-powered Arduino Nano + NRF24 handheld remote
- `Octopus_Board`: always-on Arduino Nano + NRF24 receiver connected to lights and Octopus board
- No Wi-Fi, no webpage, no JSON, no dynamic heap-heavy data path

Common NRF24 Wiring
- Both boards use the same NRF24 SPI pins:
  - D9  -> CE
  - D10 -> CSN
  - D11 -> MOSI
  - D12 -> MISO
  - D13 -> SCK
- Power:
  - NRF24 VCC -> 3.3V only
  - NRF24 GND -> GND
- Add a local capacitor close to the NRF24 module:
  - recommended: 10uF to 47uF across 3.3V and GND

Remote_Control Nano
- D8  -> status LED output
- A0  -> Lights On button
- A1  -> Lights Off button
- A2  -> Pos1 button
- A3  -> Pos2 button
- A4  -> Random Position button
- A5  -> Dimmer button
- D2  -> Spare button
- Buttons use `INPUT_PULLUP` and are active-low:
  - one side of button to pin
  - other side of button to GND

Remote low-power behavior
- Remote enters AVR deep sleep after 5 seconds of no user activity
- Any button press wakes it through pin-change interrupt
- Wake event is latched so a short press is not lost during wake-up
- Radio command retry is automatic while awake
- Sleep entry powers down the NRF24 and disables ADC, SPI, timers, TWI, and USART before AVR power-down sleep
- Status LED is mapped on D8 but disabled in firmware by default to avoid wasting battery

Octopus_Board Nano
- D3  -> PWM output for all 6 lamps through transistor / MOSFET driver
- D4  -> Octopus RESET line
- D5  -> status LED output
- D0/RX and D1/TX -> Octopus board serial link at 115200 baud

Octopus RESET wiring
- D4 must only pull the Octopus RESET line low
- Firmware releases the line by switching D4 to input mode
- Wiring:
  - Nano D4 -> Octopus RESET
  - Nano GND -> Octopus GND
- Do not drive RESET high from the Nano

Safety / long-term design notes
- Remote firmware uses no `String` and no JSON
- Receiver firmware uses no `String` and no JSON
- Both Nano boards use watchdog reset for self-recovery
- Receiver stages recovery in this order:
  - radio receive continues independently
  - Octopus serial retry
  - UART reinit
  - hardware reset pulse on Octopus RESET pin
- Receiver stops dimming automatically if dimmer keepalive packets stop arriving

Power notes for Remote_Control
- For best battery life on a Nano:
  - remove or avoid power LED if your Nano clone has one
  - use a low-quiescent-current 3.3V regulator for the NRF24
  - cheap Nano regulators often waste a lot of power
- The firmware now follows the old transmitter's aggressive sleep timing while keeping the new watchdog and fixed-packet recovery design
- Deep sleep helps, but board hardware choice still matters a lot
